---

<Doc
  title="Accounts and organisations"
  description="Accounts, organisations, roles, single sign-on with any OpenID Connect provider, SCIM provisioning, two-step log-in and per-organisation quotas, on the hosted mcpd and on your own cluster."
  lede="People log in, belong to organisations, and reach projects through a role."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Two ways to turn it on</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--signup</code> lets anyone create an account. This is how the hosted
    mcpd at mcpd.sh runs. <code>--accounts</code> turns on the same accounts,
    organisations, roles, single sign-on and SCIM without public sign-up, so a
    deployment on your own cluster lets people in by invitation, by single
    sign-on or by SCIM. In both, configured keys keep working beside accounts,
    and a request with no credential is refused.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="your own cluster, with single sign-on from the config file"
    command={`mcpd serve --accounts --config-file mcpd.json \\
  --public-url https://mcpd.example.com \\
  --smtp-host smtp.example.com --smtp-from no-reply@example.com`}
  />

  <h2>Organisations and roles</h2>
  <p>
    Every account project belongs to an organisation. An account made by
    sign-up gets an organisation of its own with one project in it. An
    organisation can own up to 100 projects, and members join it by
    invitation. Each member has one role, and the role maps onto the six key
    scopes on every project the organisation owns:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Role</th><th>Scopes</th><th>Manages the organisation</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>viewer</code></td><td><code>read</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>member</code></td><td><code>ingest</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>execute</code>, <code>worker</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>admin</code></td><td>all six</td><td>members below owner, projects, keys, SSO, SCIM</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>owner</code></td><td>all six</td><td>everything, including owners, quotas and deleting it</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A project role replaces a viewer's or member's role on one project:
    <code>none</code>, <code>viewer</code>, <code>member</code> or
    <code>admin</code>. Admins and owners keep their reach on every project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A session carries the scopes the account's role allows on each project of
    its organisations. An API key carries one scope on one project, and works
    only while the role of the account that made it still allows that scope,
    so demoting or removing a member disarms their keys at once. Nothing an
    account holds reaches a project of an organisation it does not belong to.
    An organisation id that is not yours answers 404, so the answer does not
    say which organisations exist.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="inviting a viewer"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -s -X POST $MCPD/v1/api/orgs/org_7c1e08b53a9f/invites -H "Authorization: Bearer $SESSION" -d \'{"email":"kim@example.com","role":"viewer"}\'' },
      { out: '{"id":"inv_3f9c2e1b","email":"kim@example.com","role":"viewer",...}', tone: 'ok' },
      { cmd: 'curl -s -X POST $MCPD/v1/api/account/keys -H "Authorization: Bearer $KIM" -d \'{"scope":"ingest","project":"prj_a3f9c2e1b7d4"}\'' },
      { out: '{"error":"forbidden","message":"your role on the project \\"prj_a3f9c2e1b7d4\\" does not allow the ingest scope"}', tone: 'warn' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The account lifecycle</h2>
  <p>
    Sign-up answers the same way whether or not the address is in use, and
    mails a link: for a new account it confirms the address and logs in; for an
    address in use it says so and offers a reset. Password reset works the same
    way. An address is confirmed before the account can log in. An account can
    change its password, which ends its other sessions, and its address, which
    changes when a link sent to the new address is opened. It can list its
    sessions and end any of them, and delete itself: every organisation it is
    the only member of goes with it, and the stored signals and runs of those
    organisations' projects are erased.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two-step log-in uses an authenticator app. Setting it up returns a secret
    and a QR code, confirming a code turns it on and returns ten single-use
    recovery codes, and from then on log-in, password reset and account
    deletion need a code. A code used once is refused if sent again.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mail goes through the SMTP server in <code>--smtp-host</code>. With none,
    links are written to the daemon's log at info level, which is what a laptop
    or a closed cluster needs. With <code>--signup</code> on a public address
    and no SMTP server, the doors that need mail answer 503 and the daemon
    warns at start, because a link in a log is one the operator could use.
  </p>

  <h2>Single sign-on</h2>
  <p>
    An organisation can log its members in with any OpenID Connect provider:
    Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, Auth0 or your own. GitHub works through
    an OAuth app. An admin sets the issuer, client id and client secret under
    the organisation's SSO settings, and registers the redirect URL shown there
    with the provider. On your own cluster the same settings go in the config
    file, with the secret read from an environment variable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The flow is the authorization code flow with PKCE, a one-time
    <code>state</code> and a <code>nonce</code>, and a secret the starting
    browser keeps, so a login link cannot be handed to somebody else. The ID
    token's signature is checked against the provider's published keys, with
    its issuer, audience, expiry and nonce.
  </p>
  <p>
    An organisation claims email domains. "Log in with SSO" finds the
    organisation by the address's domain, and a new account is created only for
    an address in a claimed domain. A person is found by their identity at the
    provider, and an existing account is never taken over: an account that is
    not a member must be invited and accept first. A single sign-on session
    reaches that organisation's projects only, and lasts 24 hours. With
    enforcement on, members whose address is in a claimed domain cannot log in
    with a password, and turning it on ends their password sessions.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the hosted mcpd an admin can claim the domain of their own confirmed
    address. A domain belongs to one organisation.
  </p>

  <h2>Groups and SCIM</h2>
  <p>
    Group roles map identity provider groups onto roles. With a groups claim
    set, single sign-on gives a member the highest role among their groups,
    or the default role when none maps. A group never grants
    <code>owner</code>, and an owner keeps their role.
  </p>
  <p>
    SCIM 2.0 lets your identity provider create, change and remove users and
    groups. Create a SCIM token under the organisation's SSO settings and give
    your provider the base URL shown with it. The service answers
    <code>/scim/v2/Users</code>, <code>/scim/v2/Groups</code>,
    <code>/scim/v2/ServiceProviderConfig</code>, <code>/scim/v2/Schemas</code>
    and <code>/scim/v2/ResourceTypes</code>, with filtering on
    <code>userName</code>, <code>externalId</code>, <code>emails.value</code>
    and <code>displayName</code>, and PATCH.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A user in a claimed domain with no account gets one, which the organisation manages.</li>
    <li>A user whose address already has an account is sent an invitation, and becomes a member when the account accepts it.</li>
    <li><code>active: false</code> ends the membership, the account's keys on the organisation's projects and its sessions. An account the organisation manages is deactivated.</li>
    <li>Deleting a user deprovisions it the same way, and deletes an account the organisation manages.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Quotas and usage</h2>
  <p>
    An organisation's projects share one ceiling on signals per minute, so one
    tenant cannot starve the others and adding projects does not add
    allowance. <code>--org-quota-per-minute</code> sets the ceiling an
    organisation gets and the most it may set; with <code>--signup</code> it is
    60000. <code>--org-retention-days</code> sets how long its projects keep
    data, and an owner may choose a shorter window. The usage panel shows the
    ceiling, what was stored in the current minute, and what was stored and
    refused since the daemon started.
  </p>

  <h2>The audit log</h2>
  <p>
    Every change to membership, roles, projects, keys, single sign-on and SCIM
    is written to the audit log against the organisation's id, with the acting
    account's email, whether it was allowed or refused. Admins and owners read
    it in the interface or with <code>GET /v1/api/orgs/{'{org_id}'}/audit</code>.
    See <a href="/docs/security">security</a> for every action.
  </p>
</Doc>
